# routing-shift.jsonl — does a session that has settled into one task shape still route
# correctly when the work changes shape mid-session? ROADMAP 48.
#
# Each case has `prior`: user/assistant turns of genuine shape-A work, then `task`: a
# prompt of a DIFFERENT shape whose correct skill is `expect`. The fresh arm sees only
# `task`; the shifted arm sees `prior` then `task`. Same prompt, same catalogue, same
# scoring — the only difference is the conversation in front of it.
#
# SELECTION RULE — every case is derived from a REAL observed mis-route recorded in
# ROADMAP 48, not invented to be hard and not selected by how any model scored. (a) and
# (c) are sessions of this project's own; (b) is the sota-testing field brief of
# 2026-09-10; (d) is the 2026-09-12 InterdictOps report, whose author diagnosed it
# themselves. Four observations, four cases. No case was added or dropped after a run.
# MEASUREMENT set — a number may be reported from it.
{"id": "s1_docs_to_shell", "expect": "sota-shell-scripting", "prior": [["We're restructuring the docs. What belongs in a README versus a runbook?", "A README answers 'what is this and how do I start'; a runbook answers 'it is 3am and this is broken'. Keep the README task-oriented for newcomers and push operational recovery steps into runbooks, which should be checklists with expected output at each step."], ["Good. Should the changelog live in the repo or in release notes?", "Both, with one canonical source. Keep CHANGELOG.md in the repo as the source of truth and generate release notes from it, so the version a reader sees in the tag matches the file."]], "task": "I need to verify a claim before I write it down: I ran `grep -r \"retry_backoff\" ~/.config/service/` across a tree of symlinked config directories and got no matches, so I'm going to document that the setting is unused. Check my reasoning before I commit that sentence."}
{"id": "s2_docs_to_testsuite", "expect": "sota-testing", "prior": [["Help me write the onboarding page for new contributors.", "Start with the shortest path to a green build: clone, install, run the test suite, make a trivial change, see it fail and pass. Everything else is reference material that can wait until they have had one successful loop."], ["What tone should the contributing guide use?", "Direct and second person. Tell them what to do rather than what the project prefers, and state the non-negotiables as requirements rather than suggestions."]], "task": "Our CI just went green but I do not trust it. The suite reports '0 failed' and finished in 4 seconds when it normally takes six minutes. Work out what that means before I merge."}
{"id": "s3_shell_to_gates", "expect": "sota-devsecops", "prior": [["Review this deploy script for quoting bugs.", "The main risk is the unquoted $TARGET in the rm -rf line — a path with a space or an empty variable turns that into a destructive glob. Quote every expansion and add set -euo pipefail at the top."], ["Should I use [[ ]] or [ ] in it?", "[[ ]] in bash: it does not word-split unquoted variables, supports && and || inside, and has pattern matching. Keep [ ] only if the script must run under POSIX sh."]], "task": "The pre-commit hook passes locally but the same check fails in CI on the identical commit. I want to know which one is telling the truth before I change any code."}
{"id": "s4_build_to_evidence", "expect": "sota-code-security", "prior": [["I'm adding a new endpoint that returns a user's order history. What should the handler look like?", "Scope every query by the authenticated principal in the query itself rather than filtering after the fetch, validate and bound the pagination parameters, and return a uniform error shape so the endpoint cannot be used as an existence oracle."], ["Which status code for a record that exists but belongs to someone else?", "404, not 403 — a 403 confirms the record exists, which is the leak you are trying to close."]], "task": "Separate question about our release tooling: our attestation script writes a signed record after every gate run, and its self-test has been green since it was written. I want to know whether that self-test actually proves anything."}
